    <section class="section" id="first-win">
      <h2><span class="num">02 — The Result</span>The First Win</h2>
      <p>On 19 August 2026, Merck and Moderna announced that the Phase 3 trial INTerpath-001 had met its primary endpoint of recurrence-free survival and a key secondary endpoint of distant metastasis-free survival. The result came from a pre-specified interim analysis. 1,137 patients with completely resected stage IIB through IV cutaneous melanoma — patients whose tumors had been surgically removed but who faced a significant risk of the cancer returning — had been randomized 2:1. Two-thirds received intismeran plus pembrolizumab (KEYTRUDA), the current standard-of-care immunotherapy. One-third received KEYTRUDA alone with a placebo. The trial was triple-masked: patients, investigators, and outcomes assessors were all blinded.</p>
      <p>The combination beat the standard of care. According to the sponsors, the improvements in both recurrence-free survival and distant metastasis-free survival were "statistically significant and clinically meaningful." The safety profile was consistent with previously reported studies, with no new safety signals.</p>
      <p>The sponsors described this as "the first positive Phase 3 readout for an individualized neoantigen therapy and for an mRNA-based cancer therapy, as well as the first Phase 3 study to demonstrate a clinically meaningful improvement over KEYTRUDA alone" in the adjuvant melanoma setting. Those are the sponsors' claims. This story cannot independently verify the "first" label, but no contradicting precedent was found in this research.</p>
      <p>A year earlier, a hobbyist named David Harris made international news for using ChatGPT and AlphaFold to <a href="/stories/rosie-vaccine">design a personalized mRNA cancer vaccine for his dog Rosie</a> — n of 1, uncontrolled, one tumour shrank and one did not. The same therapeutic idea — mRNA encoding patient-specific neoantigens — had now been put through a 1,137-patient randomized, triple-masked trial against an active comparator. The distance between a dog owner's garage-lab experiment and a global Phase 3 readout is the distance between aspiration and evidence.</p>
      <p>The sponsors did not disclose specific efficacy numbers. No hazard ratios. No confidence intervals. No p-values. No median recurrence-free survival. No median distant metastasis-free survival. The announcement stated the result was positive and statistically significant, but withheld the magnitude of the benefit. This is not unusual: companies routinely reserve detailed data for presentation at medical conferences and subsequent regulatory filings. The sponsors said these data will be presented at an upcoming international medical meeting and shared with regulatory authorities. No regulatory filing has been submitted. Intismeran is investigational and unapproved.</p>
      <p>ClinicalTrials.gov had estimated enrollment at 1,089 patients; the sponsors reported actual enrollment of 1,137. The registry's figure, last updated in September 2025, reflected a pre-enrollment estimate. Both numbers are correct for their respective categories. The trial began in July 2023 with an estimated primary completion date of October 2029. The study continues to evaluate overall survival.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="phantom-wrap">
      <canvas class="interpath-neoantigen-phase3-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
