    <p class="canvas-note"><strong>The Cut</strong> — A field of candidate mutations sits undifferentiated. A selection resolves thirty-four of them in violet. Then a second, equally plausible thirty-four resolves in gold beside it. Both hold. Neither is marked correct. The field dissolves and the cut is made again, differently.</p>

    <section class="section" id="two-numbers">
      <h2><span class="num">03 — The Evidence</span>Five Years, Two Numbers</h2>
      <p>The Phase 3 result did not arrive in isolation. Two months before the INTerpath-001 topline, at the 2026 ASCO Annual Meeting, the sponsors presented five-year follow-up data from the earlier, smaller Phase 2b trial KEYNOTE-942. That trial enrolled patients with resected stage IIIB–IV melanoma — a narrower and higher-risk population than the Phase 3.</p>
      <p>The Phase 2b five-year data showed a 49% reduction in the risk of recurrence or death (HR=0.51; 95% CI 0.294–0.887) and a 59% reduction in the risk of distant metastasis or death (HR=0.411; 95% CI 0.200–0.843) for the intismeran-plus-KEYTRUDA combination versus KEYTRUDA alone. These are Phase 2b figures from KEYNOTE-942. They are not Phase 3 figures. The Phase 3 efficacy numbers remain undisclosed.</p>
      <p>The distinction matters because Phase 2b trials are smaller, enroll a more selected population, and are more susceptible to chance findings. INTerpath-001's larger, broader Phase 3 population — 1,137 patients across stages IIB through IV, rather than the Phase 2b's focus on stages IIIB–IV — provides a harder test. The Phase 3 met its endpoints, but until the sponsors release the actual numbers, how large the benefit is, how precise the estimate is, and how it compares to the Phase 2b effect are all unknown.</p>
      <p>Behind these two trials sits a larger bet. The INTerpath clinical development program spans nine Phase 2 and Phase 3 trials across four cancer types: melanoma, non-small cell lung cancer, bladder cancer, and renal cell carcinoma. The same platform. The same proprietary algorithm making the neoantigen selection for every patient in every trial.</p>

