    <section class="section" id="terminology">
      <h2><span class="num">04 — The Language</span>What Was Said and What Was Not</h2>
      <p>The press release describes intismeran as a therapy "designed and produced using a patient's tumor sample to identify the unique mutational signature, or 'fingerprint,' of their cancer." The backgrounder uses similar language: "This information is then used to create a personalized synthetic mRNA coding for up to 34 neoantigens." The therapy is presented in biological terms. The tumor has a fingerprint. The vaccine is designed from it. The neoantigens are created.</p>
      <p>What is absent from both documents is any reference to the computational process that makes this possible. Neither the Phase 3 press release nor the INTerpath clinical-program backgrounder contains the words algorithm, artificial intelligence, machine learning, computational, bioinformatic, software, or AI. The word "predict" appears in both press releases only in the KEYTRUDA prescribing-information boilerplate — "predict a patient's likelihood of benefitting from treatment with KEYTRUDA" — not in any description of the neoantigen selection process. The backgrounder does not contain the word "predict" at all.</p>

      <div class="inp-term-audit">
