        </div>
        <div class="inp-term-col">
          <div class="inp-term-header">Terms absent from Phase 3 announcement</div>
          <div class="inp-term-item">algorithm</div>
          <div class="inp-term-item">artificial intelligence</div>
          <div class="inp-term-item">machine learning</div>
          <div class="inp-term-item">computational</div>
          <div class="inp-term-item">bioinformatic</div>
          <div class="inp-term-item">software</div>
          <div class="inp-term-item">AI</div>
          <div class="inp-term-item">predict (in neoantigen context)</div>
        </div>
        <div class="inp-term-scope">Scope: Merck Phase 3 press release (19 Aug 2026) and INTerpath clinical-program backgrounder. The word "algorithmically" appears once in a separate Merck ASCO 2026 release about Phase 2b data. "Proprietary algorithm" appears on Moderna's INT explainer blog.</div>
      </div>

      <p>This is worth stating precisely because it is easy to overstate. The word "algorithm" is not absent from all Merck and Moderna materials about intismeran. The adverb "algorithmically" appears in a separate Merck press release covering ASCO 2026 portfolio highlights — "the algorithmically derived and RNA-encoded neoantigen sequences" — in reference to the Phase 2b KEYNOTE-942 data, not the Phase 3 result. Moderna's own INT explainer blog uses the phrase "proprietary algorithm" directly. The absence is specific to the Phase 3 announcement and the clinical-program backgrounder — the two documents that declared victory.</p>
      <p>Not naming a proprietary component in a topline press release is ordinary industry practice. Withholding efficacy numbers until a medical meeting is the normal scientific sequence. Neither omission, on its own, is unusual. Taken together, though, they mean the public announcement of a 1,137-patient Phase 3 result described the therapy entirely in biological language, without reference to the computational system that decides what goes into each dose.</p>
      <p>Independent experts responded to the topline result through the Science Media Centre. Dr. Yin Wu, a Wellcome Trust Career Development Fellow at King's College London with no declared conflict of interest, said: "This is the first phase 3 study to demonstrate improved recurrence-free survival and distant metastasis-free survival for a combination adjuvant treatment over anti-PD-1 adjuvant therapy in melanoma which is significant in itself." Prof. Marco Gerlinger, who is a principal investigator on a BioNTech neoantigen vaccine trial, said: "I think it is fair to call it a breakthrough in the development of new cancer immunotherapies as it is the first phase 3 study that shows that a vaccine can protect against recurrences of one of the deadliest cancer types." Dr. Lennard Lee, who advises the UK government on cancer vaccines and leads academic cancer vaccine research programmes, called the findings "significant" and noted that this is "the first positive Phase III trial of an individualised neoantigen therapy and an mRNA-based cancer treatment." Sam Barrell, CEO of LifeArc — an organization that receives royalty income from pembrolizumab — said: "These results are encouraging because they suggest treatments made for an individual patient, even in common conditions, may be moving closer to everyday clinical use."</p>
      <p>None of these experts commented on the algorithm or its absence from the announcement.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="untested-selector">
      <h2><span class="num">05 — The Gap</span>The Untested Selector</h2>
      <p>INTerpath-001 compared intismeran plus KEYTRUDA against KEYTRUDA alone. There was no arm testing intismeran with a different neoantigen selection method. No arm with randomly selected neoantigens. No arm with a competing prediction pipeline. The trial has two arms, and the ClinicalTrials.gov registry confirms this design: randomized, parallel, triple-masked.</p>
      <p>A positive result from this design demonstrates that the complete intismeran therapy — the algorithm's selections, the mRNA encoding, the lipid nanoparticle delivery, the dosing schedule, the combination with pembrolizumab — adds benefit over pembrolizumab alone. This is the story's own analytical observation, not a claim made by the sponsors: that design cannot isolate how much of the benefit comes from the proprietary algorithm's selection of which neoantigens to target, versus the mRNA platform itself, versus the immune system's response to any set of 34 neoantigens from a mutation-rich tumor. The trial was designed to answer the regulatory question — does the therapy work? — and it answered it. It was not designed to answer the scientific question of whether the selector is optimal.</p>
      <p>The algorithm has never been independently benchmarked against other neoantigen prediction pipelines. Open-source tools for neoantigen prediction exist — pVACtools, NetMHCpan, MuPeXI, among others. Whether the proprietary algorithm outperforms, matches, or underperforms these alternatives is unknown. No comparison exists in the public record.</p>
      <p>The algorithm's only published performance metric — the 41% capture rate of TIL-reactive neoantigens from the 2020 AACR abstract — was measured on 29 colorectal tumors, not melanoma, by Moderna's own team, six years ago. Whether 41% is good, bad, or average is unknowable without a benchmark. And the number may no longer describe the current algorithm at all.</p>
      <p>The <em>Lancet</em> paper that published the Phase 2b primary results describes the vaccine as encoding up to 34 neoantigens designed from whole-exome and RNA sequencing of patient tumor and blood samples. It does not publish the selection algorithm's methodology, training data, or performance metrics. The peer-reviewed literature describes what the algorithm does. It does not describe how.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="threshold">
      <h2><span class="num">06 — The Limit</span>The Proprietary Threshold</h2>
      <p>A therapy that works is moving toward regulatory filing. The INTerpath program spans nine trials across four cancer types.</p>
      <p>The result is real. The trial was rigorous — randomized, triple-masked, against an active standard-of-care comparator, with 1,137 patients. The therapy beat pembrolizumab alone on both recurrence-free survival and distant metastasis-free survival at an interim analysis. Patients may live longer because of it. These are not small things.</p>
      <p>The honest limit is structural. The component that makes each vaccine unique — the selector that chooses 34 neoantigens from hundreds of candidates in a patient's tumor — has cleared a regulatory threshold as part of a bundled therapy without ever being independently examined, published in full, or compared to alternatives. The vaccine works by choosing. The trial proved the vaccine works. It did not, and was not designed to, prove the choosing is optimal.</p>
    </section>

  </div>
</StoryLayout>

<script>
  (() => {
    const canvas = document.querySelector('.interpath-neoantigen-phase3-canvas');
    if (!(canvas instanceof HTMLCanvasElement)) return;
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
    if (!ctx) return;

    const styles = getComputedStyle(document.documentElement);
    const bg = styles.getPropertyValue('--bg').trim() || '#f5f0e8';
    const PICK_A = '139,92,246';   // violet — the selection that was made
    const PICK_B = '200,150,62';   // gold — the selection that was not

    const FIELD = 300;      // candidate marks
    const PICK = 34;        // neoantigens encoded
    const CYCLE = 1020;     // ~17s at 60fps

    let w = 0, h = 0, dpr = 1;
    let field: { x: number; y: number; a: number; len: number; dim: number }[] = [];
    let setA: number[] = [], setB: number[] = [];
    let frame = 0, raf = 0, visible = true;

    const reduce = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');

    const rnd = (a: number, b: number) => a + Math.random() * (b - a);

    function buildField() {
      field = [];
      const cx = w / 2, cy = h / 2;
      const rx = w * 0.44, ry = h * 0.42;
      for (let i = 0; i < FIELD; i++) {
        // rejection-sample into an ellipse so the field reads as a cloud, not a rectangle
        let x, y, t = 0;
        do {
          x = rnd(-1, 1); y = rnd(-1, 1); t++;
        } while (x * x + y * y > 1 && t < 12);
        field.push({
          x: cx + x * rx,
          y: cy + y * ry,
          a: rnd(0, Math.PI),
          len: rnd(3, 7),
          dim: rnd(0.35, 1)
        });
      }
    }

    function chooseSets() {
      const idx = field.map((_, i) => i);
      for (let i = idx.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
        const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
        [idx[i], idx[j]] = [idx[j], idx[i]];
      }
      setA = idx.slice(0, PICK);
      setB = idx.slice(PICK, PICK * 2);
    }

    function resize() {
      dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2);
      const r = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
      w = r.width; h = r.height;
      canvas.width = Math.max(1, Math.round(w * dpr));
      canvas.height = Math.max(1, Math.round(h * dpr));
      ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);
      buildField();
      chooseSets();
    }

    // eased 0->1 ramp
    const ramp = (t: number, s: number, e: number) => {
      if (t <= s) return 0;
      if (t >= e) return 1;
      const p = (t - s) / (e - s);
      return p * p * (3 - 2 * p);
    };

    function drawMark(m: { x: number; y: number; a: number; len: number; dim: number }, alpha: number, rgb: string | null, glow: number) {
      const dx = Math.cos(m.a) * m.len, dy = Math.sin(m.a) * m.len;
      if (glow > 0) {
        ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(${rgb},${alpha * 0.22 * glow})`;
        ctx.lineWidth = 5;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(m.x - dx, m.y - dy);
        ctx.lineTo(m.x + dx, m.y + dy);
        ctx.stroke();
      }
      ctx.strokeStyle = rgb ? `rgba(${rgb},${alpha})` : `rgba(26,26,23,${alpha})`;
      ctx.lineWidth = rgb ? 1.7 : 1;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.moveTo(m.x - dx, m.y - dy);
      ctx.lineTo(m.x + dx, m.y + dy);
      ctx.stroke();
    }

    function drawThreads(set: number[], alpha: number, rgb: string) {
      if (alpha <= 0.01) return;
      ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(${rgb},${alpha * 0.3})`;
      ctx.lineWidth = 0.7;
      ctx.beginPath();
      for (let i = 1; i < set.length; i++) {
        const p = field[set[i - 1]], q = field[set[i]];
        ctx.moveTo(p.x, p.y);
        ctx.lineTo(q.x, q.y);
      }
      ctx.stroke();
    }

    function render(f: number) {
      const t = f / CYCLE;
      ctx.fillStyle = bg;
      ctx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);

      // the undifferentiated field
      const fieldFade = 1 - 0.45 * ramp(t, 0.16, 0.42);
      for (const m of field) drawMark(m, 0.1 * m.dim * fieldFade, null, 0);

      // selection A resolves, then selection B resolves beside it
      const aIn = ramp(t, 0.16, 0.36) * (1 - ramp(t, 0.86, 0.98));
      const bIn = ramp(t, 0.42, 0.62) * (1 - ramp(t, 0.86, 0.98));

      // neither resolves into a verdict — they breathe against each other
      const contest = ramp(t, 0.62, 0.70) * (1 - ramp(t, 0.86, 0.98));
      const beat = Math.sin(t * Math.PI * 2 * 5);
      const pulseA = 1 + contest * 0.30 * beat;
      const pulseB = 1 - contest * 0.30 * beat;

      drawThreads(setA, aIn * pulseA, PICK_A);
      drawThreads(setB, bIn * pulseB, PICK_B);

      setA.forEach((i, n) => {
        const stag = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, aIn * PICK - n * 0.6));
        drawMark(field[i], Math.min(1, 0.78 * aIn * pulseA) * stag, PICK_A, aIn * pulseA);
      });
      setB.forEach((i, n) => {
        const stag = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, bIn * PICK - n * 0.6));
        drawMark(field[i], Math.min(1, 0.78 * bIn * pulseB) * stag, PICK_B, bIn * pulseB);
      });
    }

    function loop() {
      if (!visible) { raf = 0; return; }
      frame++;
      if (frame % CYCLE === 0) chooseSets();   // the cut is made again, differently
      render(frame % CYCLE);
      raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop);
    }

    function start() {
      if (reduce.matches) { render(Math.round(CYCLE * 0.74)); return; }
      if (!raf) raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop);
    }
    function stop() {
      if (raf) { cancelAnimationFrame(raf); raf = 0; }
    }

    const ro = new ResizeObserver(() => {
      resize();
      if (reduce.matches) render(Math.round(CYCLE * 0.74));
    });
    ro.observe(canvas);

    const io = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
      visible = entries[0].isIntersecting;
      if (visible) start(); else stop();
    }, { threshold: 0 });
    io.observe(canvas);

    resize();
    if (reduce.matches) render(Math.round(CYCLE * 0.74));

    window.addEventListener('beforeunload', () => { stop(); ro.disconnect(); io.disconnect(); });
  })();
</script>
